.TH EUCLID-CLI-EDS-UPDATE-RECORD 1 "November 2026" "euclid-cli 2.3.2" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eds-update-record \- change part of a record set
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eds update-record
.BR \-\-zone | \-z
.I ZONE
[\fB\-\-name\fR|\fB\-n\fR \fINAME\fR]
.BR \-\-type | \-t
.I TYPE
[\fB\-\-ttl\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-add\-value\fR|\fB\-a\fR \fIVALUE\fR...]
[\fB\-\-remove\-value\fR|\fB\-r\fR \fIVALUE\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Changes part of an existing record set \- every record of one type at
one name \- without resending the rest of it: its TTL, values removed,
and values added. At least one of the three has to be given.
.PP
This is the counterpart of
.BR euclid-cli-eds-put-record (1),
which replaces the whole set. Use this one to take one address out of
rotation, add another, or change how long the set is cached, without
having to know or repeat what else is there.
.PP
.B Removals happen before additions,
so one call can swap one value for another \- which is the only way to
change a CNAME here, since it has exactly one value at all times.
.PP
.B A value to remove has to be there.
One that is not is refused, and nothing is changed: a typo that removed
nothing and reported success would leave the address you meant to take
out still being served. A value to add that is there already is not
added a second time.
.PP
Values are compared as the server encodes them, not as they are typed:
.I "10 MAIL.Example.COM."
matches a stored
.IR "10 mail.example.com" .
.PP
The change cannot leave the set empty \- that is
.BR euclid-cli-eds-delete-record (1)
\- or a CNAME with more or fewer than one value. Every value is checked
the way
.BR euclid-cli-eds-put-record (1)
checks it, and one that cannot be encoded refuses the whole call.
.PP
The set has to exist;
.BR euclid-cli-eds-put-record (1)
creates it. The server answers with the change within a moment, and the
zone's SOA serial moves.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zone " " \fIZONE\fR
The zone's apex. Required.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
The record's name, written as for
.BR euclid-cli-eds-put-record (1).
Defaults to the apex.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " " \fITYPE\fR
The record type. Required.
.TP
.BR \-\-ttl " " \fISECONDS\fR
New TTL.
.I 0
puts the set back to the zone's TTL.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-add\-value " " \fIVALUE\fR
A value to add, in the form
.BR euclid-cli-eds-put-record (1)
describes. Repeat for several.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-remove\-value " " \fIVALUE\fR
A value to remove. Repeat for several.
.SH OUTPUT
The record set as now stored, as JSON:
.IR zone ,
.IR name ,
.IR type ,
.IR ttl ,
.IR values ,
.I created
and
.IR modified .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The record set was changed.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing to change was given, no such zone or no such record set (HTTP
404), a value to remove is not in the set, a value cannot be encoded,
the change would empty the set or leave a CNAME with other than one
value, or a grant does not allow it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Taking one address out of rotation, and putting a new one in:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eds update-record -z corp.example -n api -t A -r 10.1.2.3
euclid-cli eds update-record -z corp.example -n api -t A -a 10.1.2.5
.fi
.RE
.PP
Swapping in one call, and pointing an alias elsewhere:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eds update-record -z corp.example -n api -t A \e
    -r 10.1.2.4 -a 10.1.2.6
euclid-cli eds update-record -z corp.example -n www -t CNAME \e
    -r api.corp.example -a api2.corp.example
.fi
.RE
.PP
Caching for a minute ahead of a change, then back to the zone's TTL:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eds update-record -z corp.example -n api -t A --ttl 60
euclid-cli eds update-record -z corp.example -n api -t A --ttl 0
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eds (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eds-put-record (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eds-delete-record (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eds-list-records (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eds-update-zone (1)
